99999999991}}, {"kind": "text", "text": "Yes", "bbox": {"x": 1105.9, "y": 1166.6, "width": 35.299999999999955, "height": 22.40000000000009}}, {"kind": "text", "text": "Yes", "bbox": {"x": 1202.0, "y": 1163.4, "width": 38.5, "height": 25.59999999999991}}, {"kind": "text", "text": "Yes", "bbox": {"x": 1317.4, "y": 1163.4, "width": 38.5, "height": 25.59999999999991}}, {"kind": "text", "text": "Yes", "bbox": {"x": 1445.6, "y": 1163.4, "width": 38.5, "height": 25.59999999999991}}, {"kind": "text", "text": "Bidard 2007®", "bbox": {"x": 108.5, "y": 1192.5, "width": 113.19999999999999, "height": 27.0}}, {"kind": "text", "text": "Cytospin", "bbox": {"x": 326.8, "y": 1194.7, "width": 74.09999999999997, "height": 20.700000000000045}}, {"kind": "text", "text": "CK/CK8, 18,19 (A45 B/B3)", "bbox": {"x": 477.6, "y": 1192.2, "width": 214.80000000000007, "height": 25.59999999999991}}, {"kind": "text", "text": "621", "bbox": {"x": 791.7, "y": 1195.4, "width": 35.299999999999955, "height": 19.200000000000045}}, {"kind": "text", "text": "15", "bbox": {"x": 907.1, "y": 1192.2, "width": 28.799999999999955, "height": 25.59999999999991}}, {"kind": "text", "text": "50", "bbox": {"x": 1016.1, "y": 1195.4, "width": 28.800000000000068, "height": 22.40000000000009}}, {"kind": "text", "text": "Yes", "bbox": {"x": 1105.9, "y": 1195.4, "width": 35.299999999999955, "height": 19.200000000000045}}, {"kind": "text", "text": "Yes", "bbox": {"x": 1202.0, "y": 1192.2, "width": 41.700000000000045, "height": 25.59999999999991}}, {"kind": "text", "text": "Yes", "bbox": {"x": 1317.4, "y": 1192.2, "width": 38.5, "height": 25.59999999999991}}, {"kind": "text", "text": "Yes", "bbox": {"x": 1448.9, "y": 1192.2, "width": 41.700000000000045, "height": 22.40000000000009}}, {"kind": "text", "text": "CK, cytokeratin; Muc, mucin; EMA, epithelial membrane antigen; NA, not available.", "bbox": {"x": 118.6, "y": 1230.4, "width": 673.1, "height": 23.0}}, {"kind": "text", "text": "cancer cells were in favour of early dissemination of breast", "bbox": {"x": 112.1, "y": 1303.8, "width": 692.6, "height": 32.0}}, {"kind": "text", "text": "useful tool to assess the efficiency of the entire cancer cell", "bbox": {"x": 849.3, "y": 1301.7, "width": 692.6000000000001, "height": 34.299999999999955}}, {"kind": "text", "text": "cancer cells to the BM.' 44 Supervised transcriptomic profiling", "bbox": {"x": 112.0, "y": 1333.9, "width": 692.7, "height": 34.5}}, {"kind": "text", "text": "migration process and should be analysed together with", "bbox": {"x": 849.4, "y": 1336.4, "width": 689.1999999999999, "height": 29.0}}, {"kind": "text", "text": "of 19 primary tumours has been reported, but this micro-", "bbox": {"x": 112.2, "y": 1368.4, "width": 692.4, "height": 29.0}}, {"kind": "text", "text": "circulating tumour cells in blood and primary breast tumours.", "bbox": {"x": 849.4, "y": 1368.4, "width": 669.9, "height": 29.0}}, {"kind": "text", "text": "metastasis-associated profile has not been further validated by independent unsupervised analysis. No pathological studies have demonstrated a link between micrometastasis detection and the recently described breast cancer subtypes. Therefore, although BM DTC may appear early, it is unknown whether they correspond to a genetically homogeneous subgroup of primary cancers. Among the mechanisms of breast cancer cell dissemination to the BM, bone and bone marrow homing of cancer cells may depend on similar molecular determinants, especially the SDF1/ CXCR4 axis.47 4 CXCR4 is a G protein-coupled receptor,4 which plays a role in the chemotaxis of breast cancer cells. This cellular response is attributed to activation of the PIK/PTEN/AKT/ mTOR signalling pathway,\" rather than the MAP/ERK pathway.50 51 CXCR4 expression in 142 primary breast cancers has been shown to be associated with the detection of BM DTC. 2 This pathway might be responsible for early dissemination of breast cancers, as circulating cancer cells detected in the blood are also characterised by activation of PIK. Epithelial-mesenchymal transition and primary tumour microvessel density may also be involved in the onset of DTC.54 55 Finally, the molecular determinants responsible for the establishment of BM DTC are not clearly understood at the present time. BM DIC could be a", "bbox": {"x": 109.0, "y": 1400.4, "width": 695.8000000000001, "height": 711.6999999999998}}, {"kind": "text", "text": "Are micrometastatic cells metastatic progenitors? Bone marrow is the host organ of breast cancer metastases, which is the most accessible tissue for analysis, as liquid aspirates can be performed under local anaesthesia or during primary surgery. It is of critical importance to determine whether DTC are (or are not) the metastatic progenitors of bone and/or distant non-bone metastasis. A negative answer would limit the accuracy of bone marrow DIC as a biological model, a target for adjuvant treatment and a marker of response. Pagetại was the first to describe the non-random growth of metastases, and the sustaining molecular determinants of cancer cell homing have been recently charac terised.52 57 58 If BM DTC are derived from the specific spread of a few tumour subclones into flat bones, their ability to recirculate to other organs would be somehow limited. Consequently, their detection would be linked to an increase of bone metastasis at primary relapse in patients, but not to that of other metastatic sites. Micrometastases retained their clonogenic and tumourigenic capacities in many biological reports.5961 Clinical studies have reported a link between BM DTC and the onset of bone metastasis, ' 8 43 strongly supporting the idea of local growth of DTC into macrometastases.", "bbox": {"x": 846.0, "y": 1442.2, "width": 695.8, "height": 669.8999999999999}}, {"kind": "text", "text": "J Clin Patho/ 2008;61:570-576. doi:10.1136/jcp.2007.046649", "bbox": {"x": 112.2, "y": 2150.0, "width": 506.50000000000006, "height": 20.0}}, {"kind": "text", "text": "573", "bbox": {"x": 1500.1, "y": 2147.3, "width": 38.5, "height": 25.59999999999991}}], "ms": 302.14}